description: The llvm target's collector (spec decision 7, pulled ahead of M2): `host/native/gc.c`, mark-sweep, non-moving, over one heap region the host hands over (`lg_host_heap`: `LG_HEAP_BYTES`, default 1 GiB, on posix; the profile's `:heap` on a board), so a heap test is a range test and static objects are skipped. Every block has a header `{size | flags, descriptor}`; the backend emits a precise descriptor beside each type (runtime structs by their `:ref` fields, arrays of refs by element, closure envs and continuation records by their `:box` kinds, the fixed frames `kbox`/`kfin`/`tryf`/`kinit` and try frames by their `k` and `prior` links). A collection runs only at the entry of a CPS function, when the allocator has set `lg_gc_pending`; its roots are that function's word and pointer params plus `lg_gc_globals` (every var global and `lg_handler`). The trigger is the flag, not the spec's per-body reservation: bodies are loop-free, so the overshoot is one body's allocation, and a variable-size allocation that no longer fits is `error: out of memory`. Freed blocks coalesce into size-class free lists. Rows: `corpus/llvm/gc-churn`, `gc-retain`, `gc-deep` MATCH under a 16 MB budget; P12.21 now runs `corpus/llvm/deep.lg` (10M live continuations) on both lanes. Finding it exposed a separate bug: float arithmetic went to the generic twin, and the runtime's `mul2` called itself without end; floats now lower inline as f64, by `prim-wat`'s rule.
